 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Target count limiter
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of active scrape targets a single job (`job_name`) can have.
+This means that a misconfigured service discovery source (e.g. a cloud API or catalog query that accidentally
+starts returning many more targets than expected) can make `vmagent` try to scrape all of them at once,
+which may spike memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The limit can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied by default
+  to all the jobs defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at the `global` section of `-promscrape.config`. It overrides
+  `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` for all the jobs.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  It overrides the `global` `max_scrape_targets` (and `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob`) on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets` is set to `0` or isn't set, then no limit is applied to the given `scrape_config`.
+
+The limit applies to the total number of active targets discovered for the job, regardless of which
+service discovery mechanism(s) (`kubernetes_sd_configs`, `consul_sd_configs`, `static_configs`, etc.) provided them -
+targets from all the discovery mechanisms configured for the same `job_name` count towards the same limit.
+
+When a job's discovered target count goes over the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to
+that number of targets for the job - the rest are dropped instead of being scraped, similarly to targets
+dropped by relabeling. Because independent service discovery mechanisms are polled on their own schedules,
+which particular targets are kept versus dropped when the limit is exceeded is not guaranteed to stay stable
+across discovery refreshes.
+
+Targets dropped because of this limit are shown with the `max_scrape_targets` drop reason at the following
+places, alongside all the other dropped targets:
+
+* `http://vmagent:8429/targets` and `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` pages.
+* `http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets?state=dropped` API.
+
+The `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="up|down"}` metrics, which are always
+exposed for every job (see [monitoring docs](#monitoring)), reflect only the targets which are actually being
+scraped, so they never exceed the configured `max_scrape_targets` for the given job. Comparing
+`vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets` for a job against the number of `max_scrape_targets`-dropped targets
+shown at `/service-discovery` gives an estimate of how many targets are currently being left out because of the limit.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
